-- Q3179: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the VIE and omit all disclosures","Consolidate the VIE using proportional consolidation","Disclose maximum exposure to loss and involvement with the VIE","Account for the VIE using the cost method"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-50, an entity with a variable interest in a VIE that is not the primary beneficiary must disclose its maximum exposure to loss, the nature and purpose of the VIE, and its involvement. This ensures financial statement users understand the risk even without consolidation.
Wrong (A): Entities cannot ignore a VIE in which they hold a variable interest; disclosure is required regardless of consolidation status.
Wrong (B): Proportional consolidation is not permitted under ASC 810 for VIEs; an entity either fully consolidates or discloses.
Wrong (D): The cost method is not the prescribed accounting for variable interests in unconsolidated VIEs under U.S. GAAP.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3179;

-- Q3183: Advanced Consolidations [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Eliminated entirely and never recognized in any period","Eliminated only in the year of the intercompany sale","Eliminated at sale and recognized over remaining useful life","Recognized immediately in consolidated net income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-45, unrealized profit on intercompany sales of depreciable assets is eliminated at the sale date. The gain is then recognized over the asset''s remaining useful life as excess depreciation (based on inflated cost) is reversed each period in the consolidated statements.
Wrong (A): The profit is not permanently eliminated; it is recognized over time as the asset is depreciated by the buying entity.
Wrong (B): Elimination is not limited to the sale year; the gain is deferred and recognized gradually over the remaining useful life.
Wrong (D): Immediate recognition would overstate consolidated income; the profit is unrealized within the consolidated group until depreciation occurs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3183;

-- Q3184: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reported as investment income on the consolidated statements","Reported as a component of other comprehensive income","Added directly to consolidated revenue for the period","Eliminated and replaced by subsidiary line-by-line data"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 810-10-45, the equity method provides a one-line consolidation on the parent''s separate books. In full consolidation, equity method entries (investment income and the investment account) are reversed and replaced with the subsidiary''s actual revenues, expenses, assets, and liabilities on a line-by-line basis.
Wrong (A): Equity method income is not carried forward to the consolidated statements; it is an intercompany entry that must be eliminated.
Wrong (B): Equity method income is not reclassified to OCI; it is replaced by consolidation of the subsidiary''s full financial data.
Wrong (C): The parent''s equity method income is not added to consolidated revenue; the subsidiary''s own revenue replaces it through consolidation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3184;

-- Q3187: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000 reported as a financing activity outflow","$200,000 reported as an operating expense on the statement","$80,000 NCI financing outflow; the $120,000 parent share is eliminated","No dividend amount is reported on the consolidated statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Of the $200,000 total dividend, $120,000 (60% parent share) is eliminated as an intercompany transaction under ASC 810-10-45. The remaining $80,000 (40% NCI share) is paid to outside shareholders and reported as a financing activity outflow on the consolidated statement of cash flows.
Wrong (A): Reporting the full $200,000 ignores the intercompany elimination of the parent''s 60% share.
Wrong (B): Dividends paid to shareholders are not operating expenses; they are distributions of equity.
Wrong (D): The NCI portion must be disclosed as a financing outflow; it cannot be omitted entirely.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3187;

-- Q3192: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Exchanging principal amounts between the two counterparties","Purchasing call or put options on interest rate futures","Buying and selling foreign currency at the same time","Paying a fixed rate and receiving a floating rate, or vice versa"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815-10-15-83, a plain vanilla interest rate swap is a derivative in which one party pays a fixed rate and receives a floating rate (such as SOFR), while the counterparty does the opposite. Only the net interest differential is exchanged; the notional principal is never transferred.
Wrong (A): Interest rate swaps do not involve exchanging principal; only net interest payment differences are settled periodically.
Wrong (B): Purchasing options on futures is a separate derivative strategy, not a swap arrangement.
Wrong (C): Simultaneous buying and selling of currency describes a currency transaction, not an interest rate swap.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3192;

-- Q3196: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Current period earnings as incurred each reporting date","As an adjustment to the hedged item''s carrying amount","Directly in retained earnings without passing through income","In OCI, then reclassified to earnings when the hedged item affects income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815-30-35-3, the effective portion of a cash flow hedge gain or loss is recognized in OCI and accumulated in AOCI. It is reclassified into earnings in the period the hedged forecasted transaction affects earnings, ensuring proper matching. The ineffective portion is recognized immediately in earnings.
Wrong (A): Only the ineffective portion is recognized in current earnings; the effective portion is deferred in OCI.
Wrong (B): Adjusting the hedged item''s carrying amount applies to fair value hedges under ASC 815-25, not cash flow hedges.
Wrong (C): Gains and losses are never posted directly to retained earnings; they must flow through OCI or earnings first.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3196;

-- Q3197: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The derivative perfectly offsets every change in the hedged item","The hedge fair value change does not exactly offset the hedged item change","The derivative expires on its scheduled maturity date without extension","Market interest rates remain stable throughout the entire reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-30-35, hedge ineffectiveness arises when the change in fair value or cash flows of the hedging instrument does not perfectly offset the change in the hedged item. Common causes include basis differences, timing mismatches, and credit risk changes. The ineffective portion must be recognized in current earnings.
Wrong (A): A perfect offset is the ideal hedge outcome, not ineffectiveness; it means the hedge is fully effective.
Wrong (C): Expiration at the scheduled maturity date is normal contract settlement and does not indicate ineffectiveness.
Wrong (D): Stable interest rates mean no changes to offset, so ineffectiveness measurement is not triggered by rate stability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3197;

-- Q3203: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The current fair value of the derivative contract","The total cash amount exchanged between the two parties","A reference amount used to calculate payments, not at risk","The maximum possible loss exposure on the derivative"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-10-15-83, the notional amount is a reference quantity (dollars, units, or shares) used to calculate periodic settlement payments. For an interest rate swap, payments equal the notional multiplied by the rate differential. The notional itself is never exchanged between parties.
Wrong (A): Fair value is determined by market conditions and rate movements, not by the notional amount, which is fixed at inception.
Wrong (B): The cash exchanged is the net settlement based on rate differentials, typically a small fraction of the notional.
Wrong (D): Maximum possible loss is not defined by the notional; actual exposure depends on rate movements and counterparty credit risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3203;

-- Q3204: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recorded in OCI and reclassified to earnings when oil is consumed","Recognized immediately in current period earnings as a trading gain","Offset against inventory cost with no separate income recognition","Deferred until the purchased oil is ultimately sold to customers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 815-30-35-3, if the futures contract is designated as a cash flow hedge, the effective gain is recorded in OCI. When the oil is purchased, the gain remains in AOCI. When the oil is consumed (recognized in COGS), the gain is reclassified from AOCI to earnings, matching the hedged transaction.
Wrong (B): Immediate earnings recognition applies only if the derivative is not designated as a hedge or for the ineffective portion.
Wrong (C): The gain is tracked separately in AOCI and reclassified to earnings when the hedged item affects income, not netted against inventory.
Wrong (D): Recognition occurs when the oil is consumed and affects COGS, not when it is sold to end customers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3204;

-- Q3207: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A cash flow hedge of a forecasted foreign currency transaction","A net investment hedge of a foreign subsidiary operation","A fair value hedge of a binding firm commitment","Speculative trading not eligible for hedge accounting treatment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-20-25, a hedge of foreign currency risk in a firm commitment (a binding agreement) is classified as a fair value hedge. The firm commitment is recognized as an asset or liability at the change in exchange rates, with offsetting changes in the forward contract recognized in earnings.
Wrong (A): Cash flow hedges apply to forecasted transactions that are probable but not yet binding; a firm commitment is a binding agreement.
Wrong (B): Net investment hedges protect the translation exposure in a foreign subsidiary''s net assets, not a purchase commitment.
Wrong (D): A properly designated hedge with documentation meets hedge accounting requirements and is not classified as speculation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3207;

-- Q3210: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An interest rate swap with quarterly net settlements","A forward contract to purchase foreign currency in 90 days","A regular-way purchase of a security settling within normal terms","A commodity futures contract traded on an organized exchange"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-10-15-141, regular-way security purchases and sales that settle within the normal market delivery timeframe are excluded from derivative accounting. They are accounted for on a trade-date or settlement-date basis under standard investment guidance.
Wrong (A): Interest rate swaps meet all derivative characteristics under ASC 815 — notional amount, no or small initial investment, and net settlement.
Wrong (B): Forward currency contracts are derivatives with a notional amount and net or gross settlement at a future date.
Wrong (D): Commodity futures contracts are exchange-traded derivatives subject to daily margin settlement under ASC 815.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3210;

-- Q3211: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The risk that the hedging strategy will prove too costly to maintain","The risk that the hedge and hedged item do not move in perfect correlation","The risk that the counterparty will default on settlement obligations","The risk that rising interest rates will reduce the derivative''s value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Basis risk under ASC 815 arises when the hedging instrument and hedged item do not move in perfect correlation. The basis is the price difference between the hedged item and the hedging instrument, and changes in this spread create hedge ineffectiveness that must be recognized in earnings.
Wrong (A): Cost of the hedging strategy is a business decision, not a basis risk concept under derivative accounting standards.
Wrong (C): Counterparty default is credit risk, a separate concept from basis risk in hedging relationships.
Wrong (D): The directional risk of interest rate movement is market risk, not the correlation-based basis risk described in the stem.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3211;

-- Q3214: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Off-balance-sheet with disclosure only in the footnotes","On the balance sheet at fair value, classified by settlement date","As a direct reduction of total stockholders'' equity","At notional amount within the other assets line item"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-10-35-1, all derivatives must be recognized on the balance sheet at fair value. A derivative with a positive fair value is reported as an asset, classified as current or noncurrent based on the expected settlement or maturity date.
Wrong (A): ASC 815 requires on-balance-sheet recognition at fair value; off-balance-sheet treatment was eliminated by SFAS 133.
Wrong (C): Derivatives are reported as assets or liabilities based on fair value, not as adjustments to stockholders'' equity.
Wrong (D): Derivatives are measured at fair value, not notional amount; the notional is only a reference for calculating settlements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3214;

-- Q3221: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Property taxes levied on all real estate within the city","Sales taxes collected from retail transactions citywide","Fees charged by the parks department for pool admission","Unrestricted state aid received for general government use"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 48, program revenues are directly associated with a specific function or program and include charges for services, operating grants, and capital grants. Swimming pool admission fees are charged by the parks and recreation function, qualifying as program revenue.
Wrong (A): Property taxes are general revenues because they are not tied to a specific program and support governmental activities broadly.
Wrong (B): Sales taxes are also classified as general revenues; they fund the government as a whole rather than a specific program.
Wrong (D): Unrestricted state aid is a general revenue since it is not restricted to or generated by a particular function.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3221;

-- Q3223: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A proprietary fund used exclusively for enterprise activities","The chief operating fund for all resources not in another fund","A fiduciary fund established to hold custodial resources","An internal service fund that tracks interdepartmental charges"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 54, paragraph 30, the General Fund is the government''s chief operating fund and accounts for all financial resources not required to be reported in another fund. Every government must have exactly one General Fund, and it is always reported as a major fund.
Wrong (A): Proprietary funds (enterprise and internal service) account for business-type activities, not general governmental operations.
Wrong (C): Fiduciary funds hold resources in trust or custody for external parties, not for the government''s own operations.
Wrong (D): Internal service funds are a type of proprietary fund for services provided to other departments, not the chief operating fund.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3223;

-- Q3224: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Modified accrual with current financial resources focus","Cash basis recognizing transactions only when cash moves","Full accrual with economic resources measurement focus","Budgetary basis aligned with the adopted annual budget"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 93, proprietary funds use the full accrual basis of accounting with the economic resources measurement focus, similar to private-sector businesses. Revenues are recognized when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of cash timing.
Wrong (A): Modified accrual with current financial resources focus applies to governmental funds, not proprietary funds.
Wrong (B): Cash basis is not a GAAP-compliant method for proprietary funds; it fails to capture receivables and payables.
Wrong (D): Budgetary basis is used for budgetary comparison schedules, not as the accounting basis for proprietary fund statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3224;

-- Q3226: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Account for day-to-day general operations of the government","Account for resources restricted to major capital facility projects","Manage the government''s pooled cash and investment portfolio","Account for employee pension benefits and related trust assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 65, capital projects funds account for financial resources restricted, committed, or assigned for the acquisition or construction of major capital facilities other than those financed by proprietary or trust funds. These funds use modified accrual and the current financial resources measurement focus.
Wrong (A): Day-to-day general operations are accounted for in the General Fund, not a capital projects fund.
Wrong (C): Pooled cash and investment management falls under the treasurer''s function, not a separate capital projects fund.
Wrong (D): Employee pension benefits are reported in fiduciary funds (pension trust funds), not capital projects funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3226;

-- Q3230: State and Local Government Reporting [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Depreciate all infrastructure using the straight-line method only","Exclude infrastructure assets entirely from financial statements","Report infrastructure at fair value with annual revaluation adjustments","Maintain an asset management system with triennial condition assessments"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 23, the modified approach requires an asset management system with up-to-date condition data, condition assessments at least every three years, and evidence that assets are preserved at or above the government''s established condition level. Maintenance costs are expensed as incurred instead of depreciating the asset.
Wrong (A): The modified approach is an alternative to depreciation; if a government elects it, straight-line depreciation is not applied.
Wrong (B): Infrastructure assets must be reported on the government-wide statements; they cannot be excluded from financial reporting.
Wrong (C): GASB does not require fair value reporting for infrastructure; the modified approach maintains historical cost on the balance sheet.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3230;

-- Q3232: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Account for restricted resources other than debt service or capital projects","Account for scheduled principal and interest payments on general bonds","Manage the government''s pooled cash and investment portfolio balances","Record assets held in trust for employee pension benefit obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB Statement No. 54, paragraph 30, special revenue funds account for proceeds of specific revenue sources restricted or committed to expenditure for specified purposes other than debt service or capital projects. A hotel occupancy tax restricted to tourism promotion fits this definition.
Wrong (B): Scheduled principal and interest payments on bonds are accounted for in debt service funds, not special revenue funds.
Wrong (C): Pooled cash and investment management is a treasury function, not the purpose of a special revenue fund.
Wrong (D): Assets held in trust for employee pensions are reported in fiduciary pension trust funds, not special revenue funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3232;

-- Q3236: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An operating lease expense recognized evenly over the term","A lease liability and an intangible right-of-use lease asset","No balance sheet recognition, with payments disclosed in notes","Lease payments capitalized as governmental capital expenditures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 87, government lessees recognize a lease liability (present value of future payments) and an intangible right-of-use lease asset. The asset is amortized over the shorter of the lease term or useful life. Short-term leases of 12 months or less are excluded from this treatment.
Wrong (A): GASB 87 uses a single-model approach requiring balance sheet recognition; operating lease expense treatment is not available for qualifying leases.
Wrong (C): GASB 87 requires on-balance-sheet recognition of lease liabilities and assets; note-only disclosure is insufficient.
Wrong (D): Lease payments are not capital expenditures; GASB 87 requires recognition of an intangible asset and corresponding liability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3236;

-- Q3238: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Restricted to government programs with principal maintained intact","Available for spending on any general government purpose","Held in a fiduciary trust for employee pension benefits","Restricted exclusively to scheduled debt service payments"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB Statement No. 54, permanent funds report resources legally restricted so that only earnings — not principal — may be used to support government programs. The $5 million principal must be maintained intact in perpetuity, with only investment earnings available for park maintenance.
Wrong (B): Permanent fund resources are legally restricted; they cannot be spent for any general government purpose.
Wrong (C): If the beneficiary were external parties rather than the government itself, a private-purpose trust (fiduciary) fund would be used.
Wrong (D): Debt service funds, not permanent funds, account for resources restricted to principal and interest payments on bonds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3238;

-- Q3244: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["In a separate column alongside the primary government data","As a subsidiary reported through consolidated financial statements","Only in the notes to the financial statements as supplementary data","As if they are part of the primary government within its funds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Statement No. 14, blended component units are so closely related to the primary government that their funds are presented as if they are part of the primary government. Blending is required when the governing body is substantively the same or when the unit provides services exclusively to the primary government.
Wrong (A): A separate column in the government-wide statements is used for discretely presented component units, not blended ones.
Wrong (B): Governmental accounting uses blending and discrete presentation, not private-sector consolidation methods.
Wrong (C): Note-only disclosure is insufficient for component units; they must be included in the basic financial statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3244;

-- Q3245: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Current period expenses already recognized on the operating statement","Long-term investments held for future revenue generation purposes","Revenue that has been earned but not yet collected from taxpayers","Consumption of net assets applicable to a future reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, deferred outflows of resources represent consumption of net assets applicable to a future reporting period. Common examples include deferred amounts on debt refunding and pension-related deferred outflows such as differences between projected and actual earnings.
Wrong (A): Deferred outflows are distinct from current expenses; they relate to future periods and are not yet expensed.
Wrong (B): Deferred outflows are not investments; they represent resources consumed that will be recognized in future periods.
Wrong (C): Revenue earned but not collected is an accounts receivable (asset), not a deferred outflow of resources.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3245;

-- Q3248: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only external direct costs of purchased materials and services","No costs at all are capitalized during the development stage","All overhead costs plus direct costs regardless of project link","External direct costs and payroll for employees devoted to the project"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-40-25-2, costs incurred during the application development stage are capitalized, including external direct costs of materials and services, payroll costs for employees directly devoted to the project, and interest costs. These directly contribute to the asset''s future economic benefit.
Wrong (A): Both external direct costs and internal payroll costs qualify for capitalization, not external costs alone.
Wrong (B): The application development stage is specifically the phase when costs are capitalized; the preliminary project stage expenses are expensed.
Wrong (C): General overhead and administrative costs are not capitalizable; only costs directly associated with the project qualify.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3248;

-- Q3250: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Aldridge capitalizes a $600,000 intangible asset; Bancroft expenses $600,000 ratably as service costs over four years","Aldridge expenses ratably as service cost with implementation costs as prepaid; Bancroft capitalizes a $600,000 software intangible amortized over useful life","Both produce identical balance sheet and income statement effects since the platform is the same","Neither creates any asset because cloud arrangements never qualify for capitalization under GAAP"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2018-15 and ASC 350-40, the classification drives divergent treatment. Aldridge''s service contract expenses $600,000 ratably as service cost ($150,000/year) with qualifying implementation costs as a prepaid asset. Bancroft''s software license capitalizes $600,000 as an intangible under ASC 350-40, amortized over useful life, with acquisition as an investing cash outflow versus Aldridge''s operating outflow.
Wrong (A): This reverses the treatment — service contracts expense ratably while software licenses capitalize, not the other way around.
Wrong (C): Identical platforms can receive different accounting treatment based on whether the customer controls the software.
Wrong (D): Software licenses do qualify for capitalization under ASC 350-40, and service contract implementation costs can be capitalized as prepaid assets.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3250;

-- Q3254: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An intangible asset under the internal-use software guidance","A prepaid expense classified by remaining hosting arrangement term","Property, plant, and equipment on the balance sheet","A right-of-use asset under the lease accounting standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40-25-16A), implementation costs for a CCA classified as a service contract are capitalized as a prepaid asset. The prepaid is classified as current or noncurrent based on the remaining hosting arrangement term and amortized over the service period.
Wrong (A): Intangible asset classification applies to internal-use software developed in-house under ASC 350-40, not to service contract CCAs where the customer lacks software control.
Wrong (C): Implementation costs for cloud arrangements are not tangible assets and do not meet the PP&E definition.
Wrong (D): CCA service contracts are not leases; right-of-use asset treatment applies only under ASC 842 for qualifying lease arrangements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3254;

-- Q3255: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always capitalized as part of the software development project","Capitalized only for software-related activities; data purging is expensed","Always expensed immediately regardless of the development stage","Deferred as a prepaid asset until the software becomes operational"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40-25-2, data conversion costs are split: costs for making old data compatible with new software (coding and testing conversion routines) may be capitalized during the application development stage. However, costs for purging or cleansing existing data are expensed as incurred.
Wrong (A): Not all data conversion costs qualify; purging and cleansing activities are excluded from capitalization under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (C): Software-related conversion activities such as building data migration routines do qualify for capitalization during the development stage.
Wrong (D): There is no deferral until operational; qualifying costs are capitalized during the application development stage and non-qualifying costs are expensed as incurred.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3255;

-- Q3256: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expensed immediately as post-implementation maintenance costs","Recorded as a separate intangible asset apart from the ERP","Capitalized if the upgrade provides additional functionality","Charged against and netted from the original capitalized cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-40-25-7, costs of upgrades and enhancements to internal-use software that result in additional functionality are capitalized. The key test is whether the modification enables tasks the software could not previously perform. Adding a real-time analytics module provides significant new capabilities, meeting this threshold.
Wrong (A): Post-implementation expense treatment applies to routine maintenance and bug fixes, not upgrades that deliver new functionality.
Wrong (B): Enhancement costs are added to the existing software asset, not recorded as a separate intangible asset.
Wrong (D): Upgrade costs are added to the asset''s carrying amount, not netted against or reducing the original capitalized cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3256;

-- Q3259: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expensed immediately as a period cost when incurred","Recorded as a lease liability under the lease accounting standards","Capitalized as a prepaid and amortized over the 3-year term","Expensed ratably over the contract alongside monthly subscription fees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40), implementation costs incurred during the application development stage of a CCA service contract are capitalized as a prepaid asset. The $100,000 is amortized straight-line over the 3-year hosting term, while the $5,000 monthly subscription fees are expensed as incurred.
Wrong (A): Immediate expensing applies to preliminary project stage costs, not application development stage implementation costs.
Wrong (B): A SaaS service contract is not a lease; lease liability treatment under ASC 842 does not apply to hosting arrangements.
Wrong (D): The implementation fee is capitalized and amortized separately from subscription fees, not combined into a single ratable expense.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3259;

-- Q3260: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["In the same line item as the ongoing hosting service fees","As depreciation expense within operating cost categories","As a separate amortization line item for cloud computing costs","As an investing activity outflow on the cash flow statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40-35-8A), amortization of capitalized implementation costs for a hosting arrangement classified as a service contract is presented in the same income statement line item as the ongoing hosting fees, typically SG&A or cost of services.
Wrong (B): Depreciation expense applies to tangible assets; CCA implementation cost amortization follows the hosting arrangement''s expense classification.
Wrong (C): A separate line item is not required or appropriate; the guidance mandates consistent classification with hosting fees.
Wrong (D): An investing activity is a cash flow statement classification, not an income statement presentation; CCA service contract cash flows are operating activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3260;
